  <title>
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">Supporting Protection, Transit, Voluntary and Informed Return and Reintegration of Eastern Partnership Citizens and Third Country Nationals affected by the conflict in Ukraine</narrative>
  </title>
  <description>
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">The current political and economic situation in Moldova is very fragile. On 24 February 2022, the Republic of Moldova declared a state of emergency for 60 days. After weeks of heightened tensions and escalating conflict in eastern Ukraine, on 24 February 2022, Russian forces launched a full-scale assault on Ukraine, with its military attacking the country simultaneously by land, air and sea from the north, east and south. As of early April, more than 4 million people have already fled from Ukraine into neighbouring countries, including more than 205,000 third country nationals (TCNs). Depending on how the Russian invasion proceeds, and especially if the city of Odessa, situated only 60 km from the Moldovan border, is attacked, an increased massive displacement of people including TCNs can be expected, towards the EU, including to and through the Republic of Moldova. This project supports the effective border and migration management in the context of conflict-induced mass displacement in Moldova (and potentially in other eligible countries in the Eastern Partnership (EaP) region), with the purpose of ensuring effective and dignified first line processing of border crossing by Ukrainian citizens and third country nationals in line with internationally accepted humanitarian border management standards.</narrative>
  </description>
